o prime revenue]\n- Probability that FY2027 $1.5T budget request passes substantially intact:\n  ~50\u201360% [Estimate \u2014 based on CSIS political-challenge commentary]\n- Probability of margin expansion from 15.8% segment to 16.5%+: ~60%\n  [Estimate \u2014 company guide \"low 16%\", raised again in Q2 2026]\n- Large M&A (>$10B) ROIC > WACC within 5 years: 25\u201335% base rate\n  [Framework reference]\n\nStep 1.5-2 Deviation from Base Rate:\nMy base case assumes ~7.3% revenue CAGR FY2026\u2013FY2028 (guided and\nframework-based), then 3\u20134% thereafter. This is consistent with base rates\nfor a prime in an up-budget cycle. Company-specific justifications:\n1. Record backlog of $42B = 1.9x annual revenue, with book-to-bill 1.2\u20131.4x\n   for four consecutive quarters [Actual]\n2. Raised FY2026 guidance twice (EPS $11.40\u201311.60 \u2192 $11.80\u201312.00) [Actual]\n3. Portfolio repositioned toward highest-priority budget lines \u2014 missiles,\n   space, comms \u2014 all growing double-digits [Actual \u2014 segment data]\n\nNo material deviation from base rates; no base-rate red flag.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\nStep 2-1 Business Understanding:\n(1) LHX is a US defense electronics, space, and missile prime with three\noperating segments: Space & Mission Systems (ISR, space, mission networks),\nCommunications & Spectrum Dominance (tactical radios, EW, spectrum\nsuperiority), and Missile Solutions (Aerojet Rocketdyne propulsion,\nadvanced effects). (2) It sells primarily to the US DoD, allied\ngovernments, and prime contractors, competing on security-cleared\ncapabilities, classified program access, and mission-critical reliability.\n(3) Its financial model is backlog-driven: multi-year contracts, high\nrecurring franchise value, and a \"wartime footing\" industrial-base\nexpansion program called LHX NeXt.\nUnderstandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2 Moat Assessment (+ Direction):\n| Moat Type | Score (0-3) | Direction |\n|-----------|-------------|--------