stimate]. [Unconfirmed for full segment P&L]\n4. Beta 0.746 is vendor-computed with undisclosed window; I used 0.80 as a\n   deliberate input for a defense prime. [Assumption]\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 1-1. Macro variable check\n- 10Y Treasury: 4.70% as of 2026-08-21, up ~44bps YoY (from 4.26%) and in a\n  rising trend. [Actual, FRED/WSJ quotes via search]\n- Real interest rates: 10Y at 4.70% with inflation near/above 2.5\u20133% implies a\n  positive real rate of ~2%. [Estimate]\n- DXY: [Unconfirmed \u2014 search budget exhausted; no reliable figure retrieved]\n- Fed stance: [Unconfirmed \u2014 no direct statement retrieved; 10Y at 4.70% with\n  +44% defense budget expansion implies a cautionary hold / no imminent cuts]\n- Global liquidity: [Unconfirmed]\n- Economic cycle: Late expansion with fiscal-led defense surge; FY2027 defense\n  budget request is $1.5T total defense spending, +44% vs FY2026 \u2014 the largest in\n  US history. [Actual, White House FY2027 request; secondary: CSIS/New Defense\n  Post via search]\n- Inflation: 10Y at 4.70% and rising suggests inflation is sticky above 2%\n  target. [Estimate]\n- Credit spreads: [Unconfirmed] \u2014 LHX trades IG; I assumed ~+90bps over 10Y for\n  its debt. [Assumption]\n\nStep 1-2. Regime classification (probabilities sum to 100%)\n- Reflation (high rates + growth): 40%\n- Goldilocks (low/moderate rates + growth): 30%\n- Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 15%\n- Recession (low rates + low growth): 15%\nRationale: 10Y at 4.70% (high), GDP growth supported by a defense supercycle\n(+44% budget request) but CPI concerns cap the Goldilocks weight.\n\nStep 1-3. Enterprise macro sensitivity (each -3 to +3)\n- Interest rate sensitivity: -1 (long-duration contracts; discount rate matters;\n  but defense demand is rate-insensitive)\n- Dollar sensitivity: -1 (mostly USD-denominated USG revenue)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: 0 (government-funded, no credit dependency for orders)\n- Economic cycle sensitivity: +1 (defense is counter-cyclical)\n- Commodity sensitivity: 0 (minor)\nNet macro sensitivity: mildly positive \u2014 LHX is structurally positioned to\nbenefit from the fiscal-defense regime regardless of rate direction.\n\nStep 1-4. Macro alignment judgment\n- Tailwind: the single highest-probability regime (Reflation 40%) has an\n  explicitly favorable defense budget (+44% FY27 request) and the second regime\n  (Goldilocks 30%) also supports growth equities. Conviction +0.5.\n- Macro does not change IV; it increases position conviction and allows a\n  straightforward (non-split-heavy) entry.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 1.5-1. Base rates (for a large-cap defense electronics prime)\n- 5-year survival probability: ~95%+ (defense primes rarely fail; LHX is the\n  #6 US defense contractor). [Estimate, industry base rate]\n- Probability of remaining profitable in 5 years: ~90\u201395%. [Estimate]\n- Probability a $42B backlog company grows revenue >3% for 5 years given\n  elevated defense budgets: ~75\u201385%. [Estimate]\n- Large M&A (Aerojet, $4.7B) achieving ROIC > WACC: ~25\u201335% base rate\n  (general M&A base rate). Current evidence: Aerojet is being partially spun\n  off \u2014 an admission of under-performance. [Estimate]\n- Platform spin-off success (publicly listed defense propulsion unit):\n  ~50\u201370% depending on market conditions. [Assumption]\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation of this thesis from base rate\nThis thesis assumes LHX grows FCF ~10% CAGR 2026\u20132030 and expands GAAP op\nmargin 11.3%\u219212.0%. vs. base rate of a 5-year survival/profitability of\n~90%: I deviate only mildly on the growth side. Three company-specific\njustifications:\n1. Record $42B backlog (book-to-bill 1.2x in Q2 FY26; 1.4x in Q1 FY26) gives\n   multi-year revenue visibility atypical of the base defense contractor.\n   [Actual, company press releases]\n2. FY2027 defense budget request of $1.5T (+44%) is a regime-level demand\n   shock that occurs roughly once per generation. [Actual/Estimate, secondary]\n3. Golden Dome / AMDT3 missile-tracking awards ($1.75B combined award with\n   Sierra Space; LHX to build 18 satellites) place LHX at the center of the\n   two fastest-growing defense lines: space and missiles. [Actual, Breaking\n   Defense / SDA via search]\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base rate shock check\n- Success probability implied by my base case (~75% for the bull+base\n  scenarios) vs. base rate of defense prime multi-year outperformance\n  (~60\u201370%): ratio ~1.07\u20131.25x \u2014 below the 1.5x red flag threshold. No\n  additional evidence required.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 2-1. Business understanding (3 sentences)\nL3Harris provides defense electronics and space systems to the US DoD and\nallied governments: secure communications, sensors/EW, space payloads and\nsatellites, avionics, and (via Missile Solutions/Aerojet) missile propulsion.\nIt earns ~$21.3B revenue (FY2025) with a $42B backlog \u2014 ~1.9x annual revenue \u2014\ngiving it multi-year visibility unusual in industrials. Its strategic arc is\n\"Trusted Disruptor\": pivoting the portfolio toward space, missiles, and\nuncrewed systems just as the US enters a defense supercycle.\nUnderstandability: High \u2014 defense-prime economics are transparent (backlog,\nbook-to-bill, award announcements), though program-level accounting is opaque.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat assessment + dynamics (0-3 each)\n- Intangible assets: 2 (heritage prime position; trusted security partner)\n- Switching costs: 3 (military integration, decades-long programs, sole-source)\n- Network effects: 1 (limited; some via SDA sat constellations)\n- Cost advantage: 2 (scale in defense electronics; ITAR-restricted base)\n- Economies of scale: 2 (R&D spread over $21B revenue)\n- Regulatory barriers: 3 (ITAR, security clearances, facility clearances)\n- Data moat: 2 (signal intelligence, sensor fusion datasets)\nTotal: 15/21 = WIDE MOAT (upper band). Moat direction: WIDENING \u2014\nevidenced by record backlog ($42B), book-to-bill >1.0 for six consecutive\nquarters (implied by backlog trajectory), and 18-satellite Golden Dome award.\n[Estimate for direction]\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification\nStalwart trending toward Fast Grower. 5-year revenue CAGR 10.6% (FY2020\n$12.856B \u2192 FY2025 $21.325B; [Arithmetic]) with EPS growth accelerating\n(Q2 FY26 +31% GAAP). Checkpoints: growth durability (backlog supports),\nmargin expansion (guided), and debt reduction (pending IPO/spin).\n\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard\n- Revenue growth: FY25 +9.8% YoY; FY26 guided +9.9%. [Actual]\n- Operating margin: FY25 9.0% GAAP; Q2 FY26 11.1%; segment op margin 16.0%.\n  [Actual, pack + press release]\n- EPS growth: FY26 EPS guidance $11.80\u201312.00 vs. adj. ~$10.30 FY25 \u2192 +15\u201316%\n  adjusted; GAAP Q2 +31% YoY. [Actual/Estimate]\n- ROE: ~8.2% GAAP (TTM NI $1.606B / equity $19.635B). [Arithmetic]\n- ROIC: ~5.5% GAAP (see Section 2.5). Weak vs. WACC 7.8%.\n- FCF margin: TTM FCF $2.682B / TTM ~$22.9B = ~11.7%. [Arithmetic/Estimate]\n- Net debt/EBITDA: ~3.3x (net debt $11.06B; EBITDA = op inc $2.11B + D&A\n  $1.224B = $3.33B). [Arithmetic]\n- Net cash / net debt: net debt $11.06B. [Arithmetic]\nJudgment: GOOD \u2014 strong FCF and growth, offset by sub-WACC ROIC and elevated\nleverage.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard\n1. Buyback efficiency: 1/3 (net share issuance -1.2% last year but +4.8%\n   3-yr CAGR \u2014 the company was issuing shares during a period of peak defense\n   valuations).\n2. M&A track record: 1/3 (Aerojet $4.7B acquisition in 2023 is now being\n   partially unwound via IPO \u2014 a capital misallocation admission).\n3. Capex efficiency: 2/3 (low capex intensity ~2% of revenue; but Aerojet\n   propulsion needs heavy capacity investment for THAAD/PAC-3 ramps).\n4. Rationality of mix: 2/3 (portfolio pruning to core is sound; timing of spin\n   poorly chosen).\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3/3 (raised FY26 revenue and EPS twice in 2026; beat Q2\n   EPS by $0.32).\nTotal: 9/15. Insider bonus: none (insider 0.42%; no CEO >5% stake).\nFinal: 9/15 \u2014 ATTENTION NEEDED on ROIC but guidance is credible.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 2.5-1. R&D capitalization [Assumption-heavy]\nLHX does not disclose R&D separately in the pack. Estimated IRAD ~4% of\nrevenue (~$0.85B). Capitalizing 5-year amortization: adds ~$0.34B to\noperating income, ~$3.4B to invested capital. [Assumption]\n\nStep 2.5-2. SBC treatment\nSBC = $0.113B TTM [Actual, pack]. Adjusted FCF = $2.682B \u2212 $0.113B =\n$2.569B. SBC is 4.2% of OCF \u2014 modest; no dilution concern (share count:\n190.7M FY25 \u2192 188.4M FY26 = -1.2%).\n\nStep 2.5-3. Operating lease capitalization\nOperating leases $0.785B [Actual, pack] added to debt. Adjusted net debt =\nLT debt $10.443B + current portion $0.673B + finance leases $0.231B +\nop leases $0.785B \u2212 cash $1.069B = $11.06B. [Arithmetic]\n\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/data assets\nDefense brand value is real but not separable from contracts; I assigned no\nincremental balance-sheet value. [Assumption]\n\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC\nAdjusted NOPAT = operating income $2.11B + R&D add-back $0.34B \u2212 tax 21% =\n$1.94B. Adjusted IC = equity $19.635B + adjusted net debt $11.06B +\nunamortized R&D $3.4B = $34.1B. Adjusted ROIC = 5.7%. GAAP ROIC ~5.5%.\nGap is small \u2014 the problem is not accounting conservatism; it is genuine\nsub-WACC returns on Aerojet-era capital. This is the single largest\nqualitative weakness.\n\nStep 2.5-6. Owner earnings\nOwner earnings = NI $1.606B + D&A $1.224B \u2212 maintenance capex (est. 1.6%\nof rev = ~$0.37B) \u2212 \u0394WC ($0.782B) = $1.68B. [Estimate]\nOwner earnings are materially below reported FCF ($2.68B) because working\ncapital absorbed $0.78B in the TTM. Defense contract growth consumes cash\n(milestone billing lags). I model this as a recurring but non-dilutive drag.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3. VALUATION \u2014 ENGINE 1 (DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nLayer 0 selection recap:\n- Archetype: B (Quality Compounder) with mild C (defense quasi-cycle).\n  3+ reasons: (1) backlog-driven revenue stability; (2) high FCF conversion\n  and light capex; (3) margin expansion trajectory. Alternative engines\n  (4/5) would mis-price a company whose value is in current backlog, not\n  optionality.\n- No archetype transition currently in progress (the Missile Solutions spin\n  is a portfolio event, not a business-model transition). [Judgment]\n- Transition watch: if GAAP ROIC > WACC for 3 straight years post-spin,\n  upgrade to pure Quality Compounder.\n\n3-1. Discount-rate derivation (WACC)\n- Risk-free (10Y): 4.70% [Actual]\n- Equity risk premium: 4.80% [Assumption]\n- Beta: 0.80 (pack beta 0.746; using 0.80 for defensiveness in a rate-up\n  regime) [Assumption]\n- Cost of equity: 4.70% + 0.80 \u00d7 4.80% = 8.54%\n- Pre-tax cost of debt: 5.60% (10Y + 90bps IG spread) [Assumption]\n- After-tax cost of debt (21%): 4.42%\n- Weights: E = 49.653 / (49.653 + 11.06) = 81.8%; D = 18.2%\n- WACC = 0.818 \u00d7 8.54% + 0.182 \u00d7 4.42% = 7.79% \u2192 7.8%\nSensitivity band: 7.3%\u20138.3% shown below.\n\n3-2. Explicit forecast (per-year; $B unless noted) [All post-2026E drivers\nare my [Assumption] unless sourced]\n\nForecast driver sourcing:\n- FY2026 revenue: management GUIDED $23.2\u201323.7B (midpoint $23.45B)\n  [Actual, Q2 press release 2026-07-29]\n- FY2027\u20132030 growth 7.0%\u21925.0%: my assumption, anchored to (a) FY27 defense\n  budget request +44% [Actual/Estimate], (b) $42B backlog = 1.9x revenue\n  [Actual], and (c) consensus forward revenue growth 6.68% [Estimate,\n  yfinance]. I do NOT extend the 9.9% guided pace past FY26; no company\n  target exists for FY27+ in the pack.\n- Op margin FY26 11.3% (implied by $23.45B rev and ~$2.65B op income; Q2\n  running at 11.1%); expansion to 12.0% by 2030 is my assumption, tracking\n  management's segment-margin target (16.0% segment) and amortization\n  wind-down. [Assumption]\n- Capex: 2.0\u20132.2% of revenue (pack FY25: 1.9%; Aerojet capacity expansion\n  raises it modestly). [Assumption]\n- D&A: declines from $1.30B (Aerojet purchase amortization runs off).\n  [Assumption]\n- \u0394WC: small positive drag (defense milestone billing). [Assumption]\n\n| FY      | Revenue | Growth | OpMargin | OpInc | NetInc | D&A | Capex | \u0394WC/Other | FCF |\n|---------|---------|--------|----------|-------|--------|-----|-------|-----------|-----|\n| 2026E   | 23.45   | +9.9%  | 11.3%    | 2.65  | 2.15   | 1.30| 0.50  | -0.30     | 2.95|\n| 2027E   | 25.09   | +7.0%  | 11.5%    | 2.89  | 2.35   | 1.25| 0.55  | 0.00      | 3.15|\n| 2028E   | 26.60   | +6.0%  | 11.7%    | 3.11  | 2.55   | 1.20| 0.58  | -0.10     | 3.35|\n| 2029E   | 28.06   | +5.5%  | 11.9%    | 3.34  | 2.75   | 1.15| 0.62  | -0.10     | 3.55|\n| 2030E   | 29.46   | +5.0%  | 12.0%    | 3.54  | 2.92   | 1.10| 0.65  | -0.15     | 3.70|\n\nBridging check vs. guidance: FY2026 FCF $2.95B \u2248 management guide ~$3.0B\n[Actual, AInvest transcript snippet: \"free cash flow of $3B\" from Q1 2026\ncall]. Net income $2.15B = GAAP (not adjusted) EPS ~$11.55... note: this\nexceeds the adjusted EPS guidance range ($11.80\u201312.00, which is non-GAAP)\n\u2014 small inconsistency; my NI uses ~9.4% net margin on revenue, conservative\nvs. guidance. The pack's TTM NI is $1.874B; my FY26 $2.15B assumes 15% NI\ngrowth consistent with Q2 YoY GAAP EPS +31% but deliberately more\nconservative than the straight-line H1 trend.\n\nTerminal: g = 3.0% (below nominal GDP, defense grows with the budget).\n[Assumption]\n\n3-3. DCF result\nWACC 7.8%; discount factors (end-year): 0.9276, 0.8605, 0.7983, 0.7406,\n0.6870.\nPV FCFs: 2.74 + 2.71 + 2.67 + 2.56 + 2.54 = $13.22B\nTerminal FCF 2031 = 3.70 \u00d7 1.03 = $3.81B; TV = 3.81/(0.078\u20130.03) = $79.4B;\nPV TV = $54.6B\nEnterprise value = $67.8B\nLess adjusted net debt $11.06B \u2192 Equity $56.7B\nPer share = $56.7B / 186.16M = $304.6 \u2192 $305 intrinsic (DCF-only)\n\nWACC sensitivity:\n- WACC 7.3%: IV \u2248 $360\n- WACC 7.8%: IV \u2248 $305\n- WACC 8.3%: IV \u2248 $265 (\u2248 current price)\nTerminal-growth sensitivity (at 7.8%):\n- g 2.5%: $280; g 3.0%: $305; g 3.5%: $340\n\n3-4. Multiples cross-check (not standalone)\n- FY26 adj. EPS midpoint $11.90 [Actual]: price $266.73 = 22.4x. Peers\n  (LMT ~18\u201320x, RTX ~21\u201322x, NOC ~21\u201323x, GD ~17\u201319x forward, [Estimate,\n  from general market levels]): LHX is mid-range, justified by ~12% EPS\n  growth. Fair: 23x \u2192 $274.\n- FY27 EPS ~$13.32 (consensus +11.9% [Estimate]): 20x = $266, 22x = $293.\n- EV/EBITDA: EV $60.7B / EBITDA $3.95B (FY26E) = 15.4x. Peer range 14\u201317x.\n  Midpoint 15.5x \u2192 $269.\n- P/FCF: on FY26E FCF $2.95B = 16.8x. 20x fair \u2192 $317.\nMultiples-implied fair value: $269\u2013$293 (blend \u2248 $285).\n\n3-5. Blended intrinsic value\nIV = 0.70 \u00d7 DCF $305 + 0.30 \u00d7 Multiples $285 = $299 \u2192 with scenario\nadjustments from Section 4 (bull: spin unlocks value), the expected IV\nbecomes $314.\n\n3-6. Reverse DCF (what is the market pricing?)\nAt $266.73, EV = $60.7B. Holding WACC 7.8%, g 3.0%, the market implies a\n2026\u20132030 FCF CAGR of ~5.5\u20136.0% (from $2.68B TTM to $3.0\u20133.1B in 2030) and\nterminal FCF ~$3.1B. My base case assumes ~10% FCF CAGR to $3.7B. The gap\nis the margin-expansion / amortization-wind-down thesis. Market is pricing\na skeptical version: no margin expansion past FY26. Verdict: FEASIBLE with\nupside skew \u2014 the market is not pricing a bubble; it is pricing stagnation.\n\nTornado (top-3 variables): WACC (\u00b10.5pt \u2192 \u00b1$55/share), FCF CAGR (margin\npath \u00b10.5pt op margin by 2030 \u2192 \u00b1$30/share), terminal g (\u00b10.5pt \u2192 \u00b1$30).\nThese become thesis KPIs (Section 8).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 3.5-1. Competitive response\n- If LHX wins major Golden Dome follow-ons, RTX/NOC will lobby aggressively\n  and use their DC influence; simulated response on LHX economics: -1\n  (mild share dilution, no price war in defense).\n- In space (SDA proliferated constellations), competition is intensifying\n  (Sierra Space, SpaceX, AST SpaceMobile); LHX's prime position in 18 of 36\n  AMDT3 satellites is defensible but contested. Impact: -2 on long-run\n  margin in space segment specifically.\n\nStep 3.5-2. Reflexivity\n- POSITIVE: strong backlog + rising stock \u2192 SBC attracts talent (modest);\n  strong currency for M&A/IPO of Missile Solutions; customer trust from\n  delivery reliability. \n- NEGATIVE: the July 30 IPO-delay selloff (-11%) is a negative-reflexivity\n  event: a lower stock price makes the Missile Solutions IPO even less\n  attractive and signals execution uncertainty to the Pentagon customer.\n  This is the live loop to watch.\n\nStep 3.5-3. Customer behavior\n- The US government is a monopsony buyer; LTV = multi-decade program\n  lifecycles (F-35, THAAD, PAC-3, GPS). CAC \u2248 zero (sole-source). Churn\n  doesn't apply; the analogue is re-compete win rate \u2014 currently strong\n  (book-to-bill 1.2\u20131.4x). As budgets normalize post-2028, re-compete\n  competition rises (NOC entering missile defense; RTX scaling up).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 4-1/2. Scenario composition (probabilities sum to 100%)\n- BULL (25%): Defense supercycle extends via FY2028+ budgets; Golden Dome\n  follow-ons; Missile Solutions IPOs in 2027 at >$10B valuation; proceeds\n  cut net debt to ~$4B; GAAP op margin reaches 12.5% by 2030; revenue\n  grows 8% through 2030. IV \u2248 $400 (DCF at WACC 7.5%, FCF 2030 $4.6B).\n- BASE (50%): Guidelines met; revenue 5\u20137% post-2026; margin to 12.0%;\n  Missile Solutions IPO completes 2028 at modest valuation; net debt\n  falls to ~$7B. IV = $314.\n- BEAR (25%): Budget normalization; FY2027 appropriation only +10% vs.\n  request; margins stall at 11%; Missile Solutions IPO shelved indefinitely;\n  net debt stays $11B; space segment margin declines (competitive\n  pressures); 2030 FCF only $2.8B; stock de-rates to 17x. IV \u2248 $225.\n\nDiscipline notes: Base case is the single highest probability; scenarios\nare not optimistic.\n\nStep 4-3. Bayesian update\n- Prior (base rate): Large-cap defense prime grows FCF at 5%+ over 5\n  years: ~60%.\n- Likelihood update from evidence: record backlog ($42B), book-to-bill\n  1.2\u20131.4x, raised guidance twice, Golden Dome win, +44% budget request.\n- Posterior (base+success assign): ~75% probability of base-or-bull\n  outcome. [Assumption-driven; the pack's research brief corroborates\n  via raised FY26 guidance and $42B backlog]\n- No negative update has occurred to date \u2014 the IPO delay was already\n  known at T0 and priced.\n\nStep 4-4/5. Expected price & margin of safety\nExpected IV = 0.25\u00d7$400 + 0.50\u00d7$314 + 0.25\u00d7$225 = $100 + $157 + $56 =\n$313.25 \u2192 $314.\n50% CI: $270\u2013$360; 80% CI: $225\u2013$400.\nMoS vs. current price: (314 \u2212 266.73)/314 = 15.1% \u2192 ADEQUATE (15\u201330% band).\nExpected 12-month price (my horizon, not 12-mo target): $314. Upside +17.7%.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 4.5-1. How many years of earnings does the current price reflect?\n- Adjusted EPS FY26: $11.90. Price/EPS = 22.4x \u2248 ~5\u20136 years of forward\n  earnings (at 12% growth). \n- EV/EBITDA 15.4x \u2248 ~6 years of EBITDA.\n- The market is effectively discounting only the FY26\u2013FY29 window, with\n  no credit for the post-2027 spin benefit or the margin-expansion path.\n- Reverse-DCF result (Section 3) confirms: market embeds only ~5.5\u20136%\n  FCF CAGR vs. my ~10%.\n\nStep 4.5-2. Spread\nMy horizon: 3\u20135 years. Market horizon: ~2\u20133 years (pricing stagnation).\nSpread \u2248 +1.5\u20132 years \u2192 a moderate arbitrage exists: the market has\ndiscounted a \"no spin, no margin expansion\" outcome. This supports a\nBUY but not a max-size one, because the market could be right if the\nFY2027 appropriation falls short of the request.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 5-1. Conviction score (total 15)\n1. Business quality: 2.5/3 (wide moat, record backlog, improving margins;\n   but ROIC < WACC holds back a 3)\n2. Valuation attractiveness: 3/3 (15.1% MoS; P/E 22.4x vs. peers 18\u201323x;\n   EV/EBITDA 15.4x at mid-range; reverse-DCF shows market pricing\n   stagnation while data shows acceleration)\n3. Macro alignment: 3/3 (defense supercycle; FY27 request +44%; Reflation/\n   Goldilocks regimes sum 70%)\n4. Thesis clarity: 2.5/3 (book-to-bill, backlog, op margin, FCF are\n   measurable quarterly)\n5. Execution traceability: 2/3 (two 2026 guidance raises, Beat & Raise Q2;\n   but IPO delay and sub-WACC ROIC are marks against)\nTotal: 13/15.\n\nStep 5-2. Conviction decay and transitions\n- Pending Missile Solutions spin: -0.5 (structural uncertainty).\n- Macro tailwind: +0.5 (Section 1-4).\n- Net: 13 + 0.5 \u2212 0.5 = 13 \u2192 Very High band (13\u201315).\n- Red-team logic gap (Section 9): gap = 8 \u2212 6 = 2 \u2192 NO mandatory\n  downgrade (rule triggers only at gap < 2). Applying judgment for the\n  IPO-delay execution wobble: round to 12/15 \u2192 HIGH conviction.\n\nStep 5-3. Sizing guideline\n- High conviction: 6\u201310% target weight.\n- Crowding discount (Section 5.5): -1%p \u2192 7% max target weight.\n- Engine 1 (not 4/5): no automatic cap.\n- Macro tailwind: no forced split-entry requirement; still scale to\n  respect event risk.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2