g budget cycle: ~65\u201370% [Estimate \u2014 historical correlation\n  of defense budgets to prime revenue]\n- Probability that FY2027 $1.5T budget request passes substantially intact:\n  ~50\u201360% [Estimate \u2014 based on CSIS political-challenge commentary]\n- Probability of margin expansion from 15.8% segment to 16.5%+: ~60%\n  [Estimate \u2014 company guide \"low 16%\", raised again in Q2 2026]\n- Large M&A (>$10B) ROIC > WACC within 5 years: 25\u201335% base rate\n  [Framework reference]\n\nStep 1.5-2 Deviation from Base Rate:\nMy base case assumes ~7.3% revenue CAGR FY2026\u2013FY2028 (guided and\nframework-based), then 3\u20134% thereafter. This is consistent with base rates\nfor a prime in an up-budget cycle. Company-specific justifications:\n1. Record backlog of $42B = 1.9x annual revenue, with book-to-bill 1.2\u20131.4x\n   for four consecutive quarters [Actual]\n2. Raised FY2026 guidance twice (EPS $11.40\u201311.60 \u2192 $11.80\u201312.00) [Actual]\n3. Portfolio repositioned toward highest-priority budget lines \u2014 missiles,\n   space, comms \u2014 all growing double-digits [Actual \u2014 segment data]\n\nNo material deviation from base rates; no base-rate red flag.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\nStep 2-1 Business Understanding:\n(1) LHX is a US defense electronics, space, and missile prime with three\noperating segments: Space & Mission Systems (ISR, space, mission networks),\nCommunications & Spectrum Dominance (tactical radios, EW, spectrum\nsuperiority), and Missile Solutions (Aerojet Rocketdyne propulsion,\nadvanced effects). (2) It sells primarily to the US DoD, allied\ngovernments, and prime contractors, competing on security-cleared\ncapabilities, classified program access, and mission-critical reliability.\n(3) Its financial model is backlog-driven: multi-year contracts, high\nrecurring franchise value, and a \"wartime footing\" industrial-base\nexpansion program called LHX NeXt.\nUnderstandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2 Moat Assessment (+ Direction):\n| Moat Type | Score (0-3) | Direction |\n|-----------|-------------|-----------|\n| Intangible assets (IP, classified knowledge) | 3 | Widening |\n| Switching costs (mission-critical, life-cycle) | 3 | Widening |\n| Network effects | 0 | Neutral |\n| Cost advantage | 2 | Stable |\n| Economies of scale (portfolio breadth, capacity) | 2 | Widening |\n| Regulatory/security barriers (ITAR, clearances) | 3 | Widening |\n| Data moat (intel, telemetry, flight data) | 2 | Widening |\n| TOTAL | 15/21 \u2014 WIDE MOAT | Widening |\nKey evidence: backlog up 1.9x revenue; classified/space program proliferation;\nGolden Dome / NOVA / NGJ program wins [Estimate \u2014 web research, unverified\nspecifics]. Competitor entry into LHX niches (missiles, space ISR) is\nrestricted by long qualification cycles and security clearances.\n\nStep 2-3 Lynch Classification:\nFAST GROWER / TURNAROUND hybrid. The L3\u2013Harris merger is being harvested via\nLHX NeXt cost savings; organic growth re-accelerated from ~0% (FY2024) to\n5\u20138% (FY2025\u201326). Core checkpoints: backlog growth, margin expansion, FCF\nconversion, and SG&A/amortization decline.\n\nStep 2-4 Financial Strength Scorecard:\n- Revenue growth (last 4Q YoY): +8.4% [Actual \u2014 Q2 FY2026]\n- Operating margin (last 4Q): 11.1% (GAAP); 16.0% segment [Actual \u2014 Q2 2026]\n- EPS growth (last 4Q YoY): +31.0% [Actual \u2014 Q2 2026]\n- ROIC (GAAP): ~6.6% [Estimate \u2014 NOPAT $1.96B / IC $29.7B]\n- FCF margin: $2.808B / $22.932B = 12.2% TTM [Arithmetic]; FCF conversion\n  vs NI: 150% [Arithmetic]\n- Net debt/EBITDA: $10.0B / $3.3B \u2248 3.0x [Estimate]\n- Interest coverage: ~4\u20135x [Estimate \u2014 EBIT ~$2.6B / interest ~$0.5B]\nJudgment: GOOD \u2014 strong franchise growth, excellent FCF conversion, but\nelevated leverage and GAAP returns depressed by acquisition goodwill.\n\nStep 2-5 Capital Allocation Scorecard:\n1. Buyback efficiency: FY2025 (ending Jan-2026) repurchases $1.154B at\n   average prices