rstates liquidity].\n4. The FY2026 row in SECTION 5 appears to be a TTM figure through Q2'26 (no revenue field), not a full fiscal year. [Estimate \u2014 inference from data structure]\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1 \u2014 MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 1-1. Macro variable check:**\n- **10Y Treasury yield:** 4.71% (Aug 21, 2026), up 0.44 pts over the past year, highest since early 2025. [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics/Bloomberg]\n- **Real rates:** 10Y TIPS \u2248 2.2\u20132.5% [Estimate \u2014 inferred from nominal 4.71% and ~2.3% inflation expectations]; restrictive.\n- **DXY:** elevated, trending higher [Estimate \u2014 no direct quote held]; mildly negative for LHX international revenue (~20\u201325% mix).\n- **Fed stance:** Neutral/Hold \u2014 IORB held at 3.65% (July 30, 2026 FOMC); no cuts priced near-term. [Actual \u2014 Federal Reserve statement]\n- **Global liquidity:** Central banks mostly on hold vs easing (RBI easing, Fed/BoJ holding). Neutral-to-tight. [Actual \u2014 NDTV/Fed]\n- **Cycle position:** Late expansion; defense budget cycle at a record high, but projected to roll over FY2028.\n- **Credit spreads:** Not explicitly held; investment-grade spreads near tights [Assumption]; LHX (BBB-range) refinancing risk moderate.\n- **Inflation:** Sticky (bonds selling off on inflation fears per Bloomberg Aug 18).\n\n**Step 1-2. Regime probabilities (must sum 100):**\n- Goldilocks (low rates + growth): **10%**\n- Reflation (high rates + growth): **50%** \u2190 dominant\n- Stagflation (high rates + low growth): **25%**\n- Recession (low rates + low growth): **15%**\n[Assumption \u2014 based on 10Y at 4.71% and rising, Fed on hold, growth still positive]\n\n**Step 1-3. Macro sensitivity of LHX (each \u22123 to +3):**\n- Interest-rate sensitivity: **\u22121** (valuation multiple compression; direct business impact low)\n- Dollar sensitivity: **\u22121** (international and FX translation; DoD mostly USD)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: **0** (government-funded; not credit-dependent for ops)\n- Economic-cycle sensitivity: **\u22121** (defense is counter-cyclical, but budget appropriations are politically cyclical)\n- Commodity sensitivity: **+1** (missile/space supply chains with cost-plus pass-through; some margin risk on fixed-price contracts)\n\n**Step 1-4. Macro alignment:**\nExpected macro impact = 0.10\u00d7(+0.2) + 0.50\u00d7(\u22120.4) + 0.25\u00d7(\u22120.4) + 0.15\u00d7(\u22120.2) \u2248 **\u22120.34** \u2192 **Headwind**.\nConviction \u22120.5 steps. The dominant macro factor is NOT rates \u2014 it is the **defense budget trajectory**: FY2027 up sharply, FY2028 projected \u221216% real [Estimate \u2014 Defense Aerospace citing OMB; secondary source].\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1.5 \u2014 BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550